[THOUGHT]  
This is a classic presentation of a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma (wdSCC) in an elderly, chronically sun-exposed individual. The clinical and histopathologic features align precisely with wdSCC:  
- Location (forehead) — high-risk UV-exposed site;  
- Morphology — exophytic, keratinizing nodule with central keratin-filled invagination (“crateriform”), a hallmark of keratinocytic proliferation seen in wdSCC (and KA, but course distinguishes them);  
- Biopsy findings — invasive architecture (ruling out AK), minimal cytologic atypia (characteristic of *well*-differentiated SCC), absence of mitoses/necrosis/deep invasion (supporting low-grade biology);  
- Clinical behavior — slow, stable growth over one month without regression (distinguishing it from classic keratoacanthoma, which typically regresses);  
- Absence of features favoring BCC (no pearly borders, telangiectasias, or basalioid histology) or melanoma (no pigment, asymmetry, or nested atypia).  

The differential was rigorously evaluated and appropriately narrowed: BCC excluded by keratinization and crateriform architecture; KA excluded by lack of rapid growth/regression and minimal atypia; AK excluded by invasive (not intraepidermal) histology and nodular morphology. The diagnostic conclusion is fully supported by histopathologic criteria (WHO classification), clinical behavior, and epidemiologic context. Final staging awaits full pathology, but the diagnosis itself is definitive based on available evidence.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma

